  "slug": "molecular-dynamics-848d",
  "summary": "Structure predictors and ensemble or free-energy calculations now test membrane, interface and ligand-binding states across three preprints.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is computational interrogation of protein structure and ligand interaction. The EBV preprint redesigns BILF1, LMP1 and LMP2 as soluble QTY-code analogs and evaluates them with AlphaFold3, ColabFold and Boltz-2 predictions. The ELMO1/DOCK2 preprint combines virtual screening, molecular dynamics and free-energy analysis to assess interface druggability, and releases two open-source Python tools for DESMOND trajectory analysis. The kinase preprint applies MCCE ensemble calculations to nine kinase domains bound to 18 FDA-approved inhibitors, reporting that minority protonation or tautomeric states can dominate upon binding. What is new is the pairing of structure prediction, simulation and free-energy or ensemble methods within individual studies. The kinase study is distinct in focusing on protonation and tautomer ensembles rather than structure prediction. The items do not benchmark these methods against one another, so relative performance remains unestablished.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-07-30"
}
